\section{Run A}
Let \(F_a\) be the net force on cart A, \(m_a\) its inertial mass, and \(a_a\) its acceleration in the laboratory inertial frame. All three quantities refer to that cart and frame. The balance is
\[F_a=m_a a_a.\]

\section{Independent rotor check}
For a different body in the same laboratory frame, \(F_r\), \(m_r\), and \(a_r\) denote its net force, inertial mass, and acceleration. Its separate balance is
\[F_r=m_r a_r.\]

\section{Plant A}
Here \(x\in\mathbb R^2\), \(A\in\mathbb R^{2\times2}\), \(B\in\mathbb R^{2\times1}\), and \(u\in\mathbb R\) are the state, state matrix, input matrix, and control input of Plant A. The dimensions are compatible, and
\[\dot{x}=Ax+Bu.\]

\section{Plant B}
Independently, \(x\in\mathbb R^3\), \(A\in\mathbb R^{3\times3}\), \(B\in\mathbb R^{3\times2}\), and \(u\in\mathbb R^2\) are the state, state matrix, input matrix, and control input of Plant B. The dimensions are compatible, and
\[\dot{x}=Ax+Bu.\]
